WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.400
Urban Dictionary defines a jump scare as a technique used by unimaginative filmmakers

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.500
as a cheap method of frightening the audience,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.500
and judging by a lot of comments on my last video, people tend to agree with this definition.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.000
I agree that the jump scare has lost it's former glory;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.500
not only are movies saturated with them, I think many filmmakers are using them completely wrong.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.900
But where I disagree with a lot of people, is that I think there's still hope for the jump scare,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.200
and people don't give them nearly enough credit for how amazing they can be.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.200
So let's see what they can do.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.700
And if you can't handle all the jump scares?

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.700
"Why you're nothing but a great big coward!"

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.000
*crying* "You're right, I am a coward!"

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.800
In it's simplest form, a jump scare is a jarring cut, usually accompanied by a frightening sound.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.200
To illustrate this technique, let's look at an example that's not from a horror movie:

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:49.800
"Oh, I should...very much like to hold it again. One last time.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.800
 

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.800
 

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.100
Now, what makes this more than a cheap jump scare?

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.500
What's important is that it not only scares, but it outlines a major plot point

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.800
that resonates throughout the 'Lord Of The Rings' trilogy.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.000
With a jump scare, we see how The Ring can turn the kind Bilbo into an evil and terrifying creature.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.900
A jump scare was without a doubt the best way to show the terrors of The Ring firsthand.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.800
So now, for the rest of their adventure, that jump scare sticks with us,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.200
and reminds us how high the stakes are, and how powerful the ring is.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.500
Where I think jump scares are misunderstood, is that they're supposed to do much more

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.400
than just give us a simple startle.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.400
A jump scare should add tension to the rest of the movie by making us distrust any moment where we feel safe.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.900
After being startled once, we're put on high alert,

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.400
and with every long pause or dark shadow we experience afterwards,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.600
there's a heightened suspense and alertness because now we question everything we see.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.000
This is where many filmmakers use the jump scare wrong.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.800
When used correctly the technique makes the calm moments of the movie scary too.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.300
*T-Rex roar*

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.500
"I think it's ahead of us."

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.500
"It could be anywhere! With the fences down, it can wander in and out of any paddock it likes!"

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.600
A jump scare should function like real life:

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.300
once you get scared once, you're scared for the rest of the night.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.600
There are great movies like 'The Witch', that use very subtle and infrequent jump scares.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.700
They aren't scary themselves, but the quick cuts and change in sound

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.000
still add to the overral suspense of the movie.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:18.000
 

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.600
 

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.000
"Boo!" *laughs*

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.800
The technique is done wrong when it's used too often,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.500
and isn't punctuated with suspiciously calm and suspenseful scenes,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.400
like when 'Silent Hill: Revelation' makes a jump scare out of literally everything, including a pop-tart.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.800
 

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.200
Actually, I take it back.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.200
You can use a toaster for an effective jump, but it's the great build-up that does the trick.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:48.200
 

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:55.200
 

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.500
Another misunderstanding is that people don't know what makes a jump scare good.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.600
Take this analysis of the technique from WatchMojo's video on the top ten jump scares of all time:

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.900
*WatchMojo narrator*: "It's a one-two punch of a scary face and a loud noise,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.500
*cont.*: and that's exactly what makes a jump scare so effective."

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.800
This is a completely wrong way to analyze jump scares.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.600
The most important part is not the scare itself, it's the thirty seconds of build-up before the scare.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.600
Build-up is what separates the good jump scare from the cheap one.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.400
Here's a simple example from 'The Sixth Sense':

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.000
the tent opens at the top left of the frame and moves to the bottom right, along with the camera.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.000
This framing guides our eyes towards the bottom right, where a jump scare awaits us.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.900
 

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.800
This is a jump scare that does't require a fast cut or even any jarring movement to be scary.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.900
It was the build-up from the slow camera movement that did the trick.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.700
Compare that to the laziest jump scare of all time: the bad guy showing up through the bathroom mirror.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.900
 

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.900
There's no time for the build-up, and it's a huge cliche.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.700
The predictable jump scare scenarios are giving the whole technique a bad reputation.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.200
The best ones should go against our expectations, not appeal to them.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.500
This jump scare in 'Insidious' feels so fresh because we unconsciously expected the scare

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.600
to come from the flashback, not from where she's telling the story.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.600
 

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.400
Similarly with this scene in 'Jaws'.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.900
We expected the scariest part to be when he found the tooth,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.400
but there's actually something much worse waiting.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.600
 

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.200
A good jump scare is a lot like a magic trick: it's all about the showmanship.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.600
Let's use the 'The Prestige' and a jump scare from the original 'Poltergeist' to outline this idea.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.700
*birds chirping*

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.300
"Every magic trick consists of three parts, or acts."

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:36.700
 

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.700
"The first part is called 'the pledge': the magician shows you something ordinary:"

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.300
"a deck of cards, a bird, or a man."

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:51.200
 

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.200
"He shows you this object,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.200
perhaps he asks you to inspect it,

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.200
to see that it is, indeed, real."

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.200
"Y'know, unaltered."

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.300
"Normal."

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.800
 

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.700
"But, of course...it probably isn't."

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.500
 

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.300
"The second act is called 'the turn'."

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.300
 

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.900
"The magician takes the ordinary something,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.600
and makes it do something extraordinary."

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:29.100
 

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.600
"Now, you're looking for the secret, but you won't find it."

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.200
"Because of course, you're not really looking."

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.600
"You don't really want to know."

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.100
 

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.000
"You want to be fooled."

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
 

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.700
"But you wouldn't clap yet, because making something disappear isn't enough.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.000
"You have to bring it back."

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.000
 

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.500
"That's why every magic trick has a third act."

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.500
"The hardest part."

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.900
"The part we call...'the prestige'."

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.900
 

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.700
Let's face it, jump scares usually suck, but don't write them off just yet,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.900
because when used right, they're pretty great.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.300
And don't worry, I won't end this video with a jump scare.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.300
Or will I?

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.300
Thanks for watching.

